# Untitled

## MOF-Polymer Composite Microcapsules Derived from Pickering Emulsions
Microencapsulation technologies are widely employed for the protection and controlled release of active molecules[1] and a diverse range of application-specific encapsulation matrices including liposomes,[2] polymers,[3] polymersomes,[4] dendrimers[5] and coordination networks[6] have all been exploited. The surfactant free assembly of colloidal particles at the oil-water interface to form Pickering emulsions also provides a reliable platform for the preparation of hollow capsular structures.[7] The resulting "colloidosomes" are structurally reinforced into robust free standing capsules through thermal annealing,[7b,8] chemical crosslinking of the particle surfaces[9] or gelation of the core.[10] The colloidosomes have semipermeable shells arising from the particle interstices, and permeability is modulated by changing the particle size and the interactions between them,[7b–9] or by adsorption of polymers, polyelectrolyte layers or particles onto the colloidosome surfaces.[11]
Colloidosomes have been prepared from particles of diverse composition including hybrid core-shell and other composite materials,[7–11,12] but despite best efforts their retention of small molecules remains relatively poor. It is therefore surprising there are virtually no examples of systems that incorporate particles with well-defined micropores,[12d,13] particularly if these are embedded in a non-permeable matrix. The benefits of such an approach are well established in separation technology where mixed-matrix membranes combining the porosity and selectivity of regular inorganic pores with the processability and flexibility of organic polymers generate composite materials with superior separation and mechanical performance.[14] Clearly this would be advantageous in encapsulation technology where the retention and release of small molecules from mechanically robust composite colloidosomes could be controlled by micropore size and functionality.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an exciting class of crystalline porous material formed by the self-assembly of transition metal ions or clusters and polytopic organic linkers.[15] Their highly regular and tuneable micropores and relative ease of preparation and functionalization ensure they have broad application in catalysis,[16] gas storage[17] and separation processes.[18] Due to their inorganic-organic hybrid nature
MOFs have flexible structures[19] often displaying dynamic guest-responsive adsorption behaviors.[20] Further, the incorporation of magnetic or photoactive components means they can respond readily to external stimuli, for example to control porosity.[21] These are all desirable properties for advanced encapsulation technologies, and MOFs are thus an excellent platform from which to introduce functional micropores into the shells of composite capsules.[22]
MOF crystallites have previously been assembled at the air-liquid interface using Langmuir–Blodgett techniques[23] and can form ordered supraparticle assemblies from colloidal solution,[24] but their wider nanoassembly is not yet well explored. There are however a number of well-defined strategies for the preparation of MOF nanoparticles (NPs),[25] and their hybrid composition, mid-range zeta potentials and facile surface functionalization suggest they are highly suitable for assembly at liquid-liquid interfaces. Here we report for the first time the preparation of oil-in-water (o/w) Pickering emulsions stabilised by the assembly of pre-formed MOF NPs at the o/w interface. The emulsions are formed by application of high shear forces to biphasic mixtures of dodecane and aqueous dispersions of MOF NPs. When the organic inner phase contains monomers, cross-linkers and an initiator these stable "MOF somes" can act as polymerisation microreactors[26] to form the desired composite microcapsules via polymer-induced phase separation of a cross-linked polystyrene (PS) membrane. (Scheme 1, Figure 1) This constitutes a simple, convenient and wide-ranging strategy for the one-pot preparation of diverse MOF polymer composite microcapsules dependent only on the tuneable surface properties of MOFs and is an important step toward the preparation of capsular systems incorporating controllable micropores. The composite capsules are of broad relevance for the encapsulation of active molecules, which we demonstrate using a model dye compound.
Initial efforts focused on the preparation of hollow composite microspheres from particles of ZIF-8 (Supporting Information, Figure S1). ZIF-8 is a tetrahedrally connected framework of composition \(\mathrm{Zn}(2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MeIm})_{2}\) (2-MeIm = 2-methylimidazole) with sodalite topology[27] that displays high water stability and flexible sorption behavior.[28] The pores in the structure are accessed through windows of cross-section \(0.3\;\mathrm{nm}\), making this an excellent test system to examine the effect of small micropores on the retention of molecules by composite capsules. Furthermore, our aqueous dispersion of ZIF-8 colloids has a zeta potential of \(+23.7\pm0.8\) (Supporting Information, Table S1) so should have sufficient tendency toward the o/w interface to form stable Pickering emulsions, consistent with the hydrophobic nature of the ZIF-8 pore structure. After screening several reaction compositions, hollow microspheres (Figure 1) were reliably produced by adding \(0.4\;\mathrm{g}\) of dodecane to a dispersion of \(0.2\;\mathrm{g}\)
of ZIF-8 NPs in \(10\,\mathrm{\ml}\) of water. The oil phase contained the styrene (Sty) and divinylbenzene (DVB) components (2:3) and initiator necessary to form the cross-linked PS membrane. The ZIF-8:organics \((\mathrm{Sty}+\mathrm{DVB})\) mass ratio in the system is 1:1.75, and this mixture was sheared at \(16\,500\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for 2 mins to form a ZIF-8 stabilised Pickering emulsion (Figure 1a, Supporting Information, Figure S2,S3) that was subsequently heated at \(65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to effect polymerisation. Hollow ZIF-8@PS composite microcapsules are readily formed as the dodecane is a poor solvent for the cross-linked PS network leading to phase separation and precipitation at the interface, locking the ZIF-8 NPs into the capsule surface.[13]
The ZIF-8@PS microspheres are controllably hollow since other reaction compositions lead to core-shell structures. For example, when the amount of organics is increased to a ZIF8:organics mass ratio of 1:2.25, microspheres comprising a macroporous PS core decorated by a shell of ZIF-8 NPs are
formed (Supporting Information, Figure S4). The nature of the oil phase is also important, and exchanging dodecane for toluene or heptane also yields core-shell structures for the same 1:1.75 ZIF-8:organics mass ratio (Supporting Information, Figure S5). This occurs as these solvents are better able to solubilize the PS network preventing effective phase separation and thus capsule formation.
Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images of composite capsules prepared with a ZIF-8:organics ratio of 1:1.75 reveal formation of intact spherical structures with a size distribution of \(41\,\pm\,13\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Figure 1b,c). The microspheres are slightly dimpled but do not collapse under the conditions of the SEM analysis, suggesting moderate mechanical stability. The external surface consists of homogeneously distributed ZIF-8 NPs embedded in the PS shell (Figure 1d), indicating that the surface of the as-made ZIF-8 is sufficiently wettable by the dispersed oil phase without further modification as is
often required to generate stable emulsions from inorganic colloids.[29] This is confirmed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) which reveals clear indentations in the PS surface where ZIF-8 NPs become dislodged during the microtoming process (Supporting Information, Figure S6). The size and shape of the ZIF-8 NPs are unchanged confirming their stability throughout the polymerisation process, and their structure is established by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Supporting Information, Figure S7). The hollow nature of the capsules is visualized with SEM after deliberate crushing (Figure 1e, Supporting Information, Figure S8).
The thickness of the polymer shell in the ZIF-8@PS capsules is \(1.8\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\), which is dependent on the amount of Sty and DVB present in the oil phase: for example, a reaction system with a ZIF-8:organics mass ratio of 1:1.25 has a reduced shell thickness of \(1.0\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Supporting Information, Figure S4). This is consistent with wholly PS capsule shells prepared using interfacial phase separation.[30] SEM images of the polymer shell after acid dissolution of the ZIF-8 show a number of pits on the outer surface where
the ZIF-8 NPs once were (Figure 2a), further confirming their excellent surface wettability. The cross section images (Figure 2b) reveal two distinct regions within the polymer shell: 1) a continuous disordered network of interconnected meso-/ macropores below the pits, and 2) a thin but much denser polymeric layer at the capsule exterior surface. This hierarchical structuring of the PS is reminiscent of that observed for poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) hollow fibre membranes prepared by phase inversion[31] and 'pomegranate' like PS microspheres.[32] In all cases this structuring arises from the nature of the interfacial phase separation, and will surely play an important role in molecular retention/release by the composite capsules.
Bulk samples of the ZIF-8@PS capsules were further characterized by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and nitrogen adsorption. TGA indicates the capsules decompose in a single step at temperatures \(>350\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Supporting Information, Figure S9), and the ZIF-8 contents is \(20~\mathrm{wt\%}\) determined by the residual \(_{\mathrm{ZnO}}\) after full decomposition. Nitrogen adsorption (Supporting Information, Figure S10) of the composite reveals an isotherm distinct from that recorded for as-made ZIF-8 NPs, most notably a modest hysteretic desorption profile reflecting the overall contribution of the polymer component. Following ZIF-8 removal, the polymer shell displays an intermediate type II/IV isotherm (Supporting Information, Figure S11),
indicative of meso-/macroporosity, and a broad hysteresis over the entire pressure range. This may be attributable to trapping of the adsorbate in small pores or swelling of the polymer shell as observed for polymers of intrinsic microporosity (PIMs).[33] While such hysteresis has previously been reported for hypercrosslinked PS,[34] it is generally difficult to assign this wholly to swelling behavior. In any case, the hysteresis observed for the composite is markedly reduced suggesting that any polymer swelling is largely tempered by the presence of ZIF-8 and the excellent interface between the two components. The apparent BET surface area of ZIF-8@PS is \(756\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), higher than would be expected for \(20\%\) ZIF-8 plus the polymer component (BET of PS shell \(=156~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\)). These data suggest the pores of ZIF-8 remain accessible in the composite capsules, and the additional microporosity likely arises from the formation of small pores at the ZIF-8-polymer interface. This is further supported by the hysteresis on desorption – although a small contribution from polymer swelling to this hysteresis cannot be ruled out – and an increased composite micropore volume over \(20\%\) ZIF-8.
The nature of the surface of colloidal particles is an important parameter for assembly at the o/w interface.[29,35] To test the generality of our interfacial assembly approach and investigate the influence of MOF surface properties on emulsion stability and capsule formation, we also used NPs of the prototypical carboxylate-based MOFs MIL-101[36] and UiO-66.[37] (Supporting Information, Figure S1) These MOFs are composed of terephthalic acid linkers and \(\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6}\) (MIL-101) and \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}\) (UiO-66) cluster-based secondary building units (SBUs), and have larger accessible pore windows \((0.6{-}1.0\ \mathrm{nm})\) than ZIF-8. Although surface properties of MOFs are directly determined by the composition of the framework, their surfaces are tunable using facile ligand exchange strategies.[38] As-made MIL-101 is more hydrophilic than ZIF-8, whereas the UiO-66 NPs used are capped either by the benzoic acid modifier used to control their particle size during synthesis (benz-UiO-66) or amphiphilic heptanoic acid (hep-UiO-66) following post-synthetic ligand exchange, permitting facile modulation of MOF surface hydrophobicity.
SEM microscopy reveals MOF@PS capsular structures are formed from both of the carboxylate-based MOFs (Supporting Information, Figure S12–S14), and while benz-UiO-66 particles are embedded within the PS network (Supporting Information, Figure S14) and thus comparable to ZIF-8@PS, incorporation of MIL-101 into the composites is dependent on the addition of NaCl to the aqueous phase (Supporting Information, Figure S12) as previously described for Laponite clays.[39] This observation suggests the surface wettability of benz-UiO-66 is similar to that of ZIF-8, but MIL-101 is significantly poorer; the latter is confirmed by TEM showing MIL-101 NPs are only deposited on the exterior surface of the polymer shell rather than being embedded within it. (Supporting Information, Figure S13) MIL-101 and benz-UiO-66 have zeta potentials of \(+34.3\,\pm\,0.3\) and \(+33.3\,\pm\,0.3\) (Supporting Information, Table S1), respectively, so will have a lower overall tendency to partition at the o/w interface than ZIF-8 \((+23.7\,\pm\,0.8)\) arising from greater aqueous colloidal stability due to their hydrophilic SBUs. While this does not prevent stable emulsion formation, it does affect the level of MOF inclusion into the composite capsules. As a result, addition of NaCl is required to ensure hydrophilic
MIL-101 associates with the PS shell but not for benz-UiO-66, which has greater surface hydrophobicity. In fact, addition of NaCl increases 10-fold the MOF content of the \(\mathrm{MIL-101}@\) PS composites: without NaCl only smooth surfaced polymer capsules containing \({\approx}1\%\) MOF (from TGA) are formed (Supporting Information, Figure S12). To further enhance MIL-101 incorporation to \(15\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) , a vinyl-pyridine monomer can also be added to the oil phase to promote interfacial interactions through pyridine-binding to the SBU cluster. (Supporting Information, Figure S12). The \(\mathrm{MIL-101@PS}\) and benz-UiO-66@PS composite capsules were fully characterized by PXRD, TGA and nitrogen adsorption (Supporting Information, Figure S15–S20). In contrast to ZIF-8@PS, the nitrogen uptake for \(\mathrm{MIL-101}@\) PS and benz-UiO-66@PS is lower than expected based on the TGA-derived MOF contents. This suggests that the larger pore size of these MOFs may allow some ingress of the polymer which blocks their pores and reduces microporosity after composite formation.
Surface hydrophobicity of as-made benz-UiO-66 can be increased by exchanging the benzoate capping groups with heptanoic acid (Supporting Information, Figure S21). While benz-UiO-66 and hep-UiO-66 have very similar (aqueous) colloidal stability as determined by zeta potential (Supporting Information, Table S1), the hydrophobicity of the latter permits its dispersion in paraffin oil and thus the formation of reverse phase capsules. The water droplets contained soluble monomers to form a cross-linked poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) (PNIPAM) membrane at the w/o interface upon heating, to readily prepare hep-UiO-66@PNIPAM capsules containing an aqueous core. SEM and TEM images reveal a number of dimpled spherical structures, and a few broken hollow capsules with a size distribution of \(23\pm9\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Figure 3, and Supporting Information, Figure S22). A close-up of the shell indicates that the hep-UiO-66 NPs are homogeneously dispersed within the PNIPAM membrane rather than being embedded only in the exterior surface as observed for \(\mathrm{MOF}@{\mathrm{PS}}\) capsules. This is analogous to the structure of MOF mixed-matrix separation membranes[40] and arises from the terminal amide groups of the PNIPAM which are able to interact more strongly than PS with the surfaces of MOF NPs.
Evaluation of colloidosome shell permeability is critical to assess their wider applicability for molecular encapsulation, and is typically carried out using dye molecules[41] or semiconductor quantum dots[42] as probes of shell integrity. To be
an effective probe of shell permeability and the MOF-polymer interface here, the probe molecule should ideally be inaccessible to the MOF micropores. We selected the large dye molecule oil red O (ORO) as a suitable encapsulate to assess ZIF-8@PS capsule shell permeability, as molecular dynamics (MD) simulations confirm this is not adsorbed by the small pore framework (Supporting Information, Section S4). While UiO-66 also does not adsorb ORO, our MD studies reveal that MIL-101 readily does so (Supporting Information, Section S4), consistent with other large pore MOFs.[43] MIL-101@PS was thus not investigated further, since ORO release would be difficult to assign unambiguously to the interface due to competing release through large micropores.
ZIF-8@PS capsules were loaded by dissolving a small amount of ORO in the dodecane phase during synthesis (Supporting Information, Figure S25). Successful dye encapsulation was confirmed by confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) which revealed that the otherwise non-fluorescent composite capsules fluoresce strongly upon ORO loading (Supporting Information, Figure S26,S27). Intensity profiles and a reconstructed z-stack image of dye-loaded capsules (ORO-ZIF-8@PS) indicate ORO is distributed homogeneously throughout the capsule interior (Figure 4a, Supporting Information, Figure S27). Following washing with EtOH to remove nonencapsulated dye, the capsules were redispersed in EtOH at room temperature to assess dye release: ORO is soluble in dodecane and EtOH which are fully miscible under ambient conditions (Supporting Information, Figure S28,S29). After redispersion of \(\mathrm{ORO-ZIF-8@PS}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) , the capsules remain fluorescent (Supporting Information, Figure S27) clearly demonstrating that the ZIF-8@PS composite retains high levels of dye. This is confirmed by UV–vis spectroscopy of the supernatant (Figure 4b) where only \(9.7\%\) of the encapsulated ORO – normalized to the polymer shell release yield of \(80\%\) (Supporting Information, Figure S30) – is released after \(12~\mathrm{h}\) . This indicates the ZIF-8-polymer interface is largely free of major defects, and SEM reveals the surface remains intact after dye release (Supporting Information, Figure S31). In contrast, ORO-UiO-66@PS capsules rapidly lose the loaded dye during the washing step, implying the interface is much poorer than expected. The low release from ZIF-8@PS capsules compares very well to other colloidosomes where dye release is typically \(80–100\%\) over the same time period, even after sintering[44] or cross-linking of hybrid particles.[41,45] Absorption of polymer overlayers can further slow release by providing a barrier to diffusion;[46] however, this is highly system dependent and a good match between polymer and dye solubility will necessarily be required.
After ZIF-8 is removed from the ORO loaded capsules by acid dissolution, dye release is extremely rapid: a burst profile is observed with \(70\%\) ORO released in the first \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) , and full release occurring within \({\approx}7\) h (Figure 4b). The polymer component is thus highly permeable to the dye (Supporting Information, Figure S32), as expected from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption and microscopy images of the polymer shell. This indicates that the small micropores of the ZIF-8 component regulate dye access through the porous polymer shell into the external medium; although some release does occur, this is most likely via small pores at the MOF-polymer interface suggesting the efficient
retention observed for ORO may not be applicable to smaller encapsulates. Dye retention must also be related to the structure of the polymer shell since complete surface coverage of the capsule exterior by ZIF-8 is not attained (Figure 1 and Figure 2). It is noteworthy that the underlying porous network revealed by SEM (Figure 2) does not appear to breach the outer surface of the capsules[32] except at the bottom of the pits where the ZIF-8 NPs reside, and SEM images (Supporting Information, Figure S33) reveal the outer surface does not suffer from pitting or etching following acid removal of ZIF-8. It is thus likely that the ORO can diffuse into the porous polymer shell but is prevented from significant release by the denser surface (skin) layer and the ZIF-8 micropores; however, if the ZIF-8 NPs are removed the underlying porous network becomes exposed and dye release is rapid through the larger PS pores. This is similar to the recently reported pH-triggered drug release from coordination-polymer coated mesoporous silica nanoparticles.[47]
In summary we demonstrate a facile synthetic method for the preparation of MOF-polymer composite microcapsules by immobilising MOF NPs assembled at the interface of emulsion droplets into a PS membrane. Capsule formation is dependent on the surface properties of the NPs and the phase separation behaviour of the polymer component. The interfacial assembly route described should be widely applicable for the preparation of diverse MOF-polymer composite capsules given the range of monomers available, reliable strategies for the controlled preparation of nanoscale MOFs and the ease of their surface modification. For example, the tunable surface hydrophobicity of MOFs permits their dispersion in a continuous oil phase so MOFs that are more water sensitive than those exemplified here may also be integrated into capsular structures, extending their utility further by allowing both lipophilic and aqueous payloads to be encapsulated. In the present case, the combination of a small pore MOF and a hierarchically structured polymer membrane with an excellent interface between them appears effective for the retention of encapsulated dye molecules. This suggests a new strategy for the preparation of advanced colloidsome microcapsule systems based on microporous MOFs, which could be further endowed with the stimuli-responsive functionality often associated with these coordination-based networks. We are currently screening suitable hydrophobic guests that will not disrupt the capsule polymerization process in order to evaluate the selective release properties of MOF@polymer capsules. Finally, assembly at the o/w interface should allow pre-formed NPs of functional MOFs to be conveniently integrated into high performance nanodevices and sensors.[48] particularly if this can be coupled with directional interactions.
